Title: Autotune the test suite's parallelism to the host's cores
Created: 2026-10-25
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-25T06:26Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

The full test gate hard-codes its parallelism, and the hosts differ: doudou has 4 cores, padme has 32.

Read in the repo: `pyproject.toml` pins `pytest-xdist` with a comment that `-n 4` measured 81 s to 38.6 s (ticket 1011);
the `Makefile` target `check-tests` uses `-n 8` (46.9 s at `-n 4` to 33.8 s at `-n 8`, three trials, load 7 to 9 on padme);
CI keeps its own `-n 4` and says the forge runner's cores were not measured; `check-fast` and `lint` stay serial. The fall-back
to serial when xdist is missing is already in the Makefile.

`-n 8` on a 4-core host oversubscribes; `-n 8` on a 32-core host leaves most cores idle (derived from the figures above, not
measured at other values). `-n auto` counts logical CPUs (64 on padme), which is also unmeasured here.

Related, from the arena (ticket 1094): on padme, test time was 30 to 66 % of an agent leg's wall-clock, and the 3975WX
(32 cores) is not faster than the 3945WX it replaced on a single core, so test time is where parallelism matters most.
The agent legs run in other repositories, so this ticket concerns the IDH suite and the pattern, not the arena's targets.

## Design

Compute `-n` from the number of physical cores with a floor and a cap and an environment override, in one place that the
Makefile (and CI, if it can read it) share. The rule is chosen from measurements on both hosts, not from this text.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Measured wall-clock of the full gate at several `-n` values on padme (32 cores) and doudou (4 cores), three trials each, recorded in the ticket
- [ ] A rule that picks `-n` from the host, with the override and the serial fall-back, and a test that pins the rule
- [ ] The worker-safety audit of ticket 1011 extended to the highest `-n` the rule can pick
- [ ] Makefile and pyproject comments point to the one rule instead of stating numbers
